From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Sat, 6 Sep 2026 04:50:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] drm/msm/dpu: Do not program PCC for an identity CTM

An identity CTM asks the colour correction block to compute out = in. That
buys nothing and costs ten register writes plus a flush on every modeset and
colour-management change.

It also costs correctness. An X1E80100 laptop intermittently tints half its
screen: layout and content stay correct, only colour is wrong, and a dpms
cycle clears it. Register snapshots taken while glitched and while healthy
show DSPP_1's PCC block enabled with PCC_RED_R reading zero while
PCC_GREEN_G and PCC_BLUE_B hold 0x8000 -- a matrix whose red output is
identically zero.

Logging the coefficients as they were programmed showed the driver is not at
fault: every programming recorded a clean identity CTM in and
rr=8000 gg=8000 bb=8000 out, with both DSPPs written in the same commit. The
driver writes 0x8000 to PCC_RED_R and the register reads back zero
afterwards, so a coefficient write is not latching. dpu_crtc.c is the only
caller of setup_pcc(), and it writes the nine coefficients before PCC_EN, so
nothing else is overwriting it.

The latching failure itself is not addressed here -- it needs hardware
documentation this was written without. But an identity matrix never needs
programming in the first place, and not enabling PCC makes an unlatched
coefficient unreachable rather than merely unlikely.

Disable rather than skip, since an earlier commit may have left a
non-identity matrix programmed.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_crtc.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_crtc.c
@@ -844,6 +844,28 @@
 	gc_lut->flags = 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * An identity CTM asks the hardware to compute out = in, so programming PCC for
+ * it buys nothing and costs ten register writes plus a flush per modeset.
+ *
+ * It also costs correctness here. On X1E80100 a PCC coefficient write has been
+ * observed not to latch: the driver writes 0x8000 to PCC_RED_R and the register
+ * reads back zero afterwards, leaving an *enabled* PCC block with a dead red
+ * channel that tints half the screen until the next modeset. Not programming an
+ * identity matrix makes that failure unreachable rather than merely unlikely.
+ */
+static bool _dpu_crtc_ctm_is_identity(const struct drm_color_ctm *ctm)
+{
+	const u64 one = 1ULL << 32;	/* 1.0 in S31.32 */
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 9; i++)
+		if (ctm->matrix[i] != (i % 4 == 0 ? one : 0))
+			return false;
+
+	return true;
+}
+
 static void _dpu_crtc_setup_cp_blocks(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
 {
 	struct drm_crtc_state *state = crtc->state;
@@ -867,7 +889,14 @@
 			continue;
 
 		if (dspp->ops.setup_pcc) {
-			if (!state->ctm) {
+			const struct drm_color_ctm *ctm = state->ctm ?
+				(struct drm_color_ctm *)state->ctm->data : NULL;
+
+			/*
+			 * Disable rather than skip: a previous commit may have
+			 * left a non-identity matrix programmed.
+			 */
+			if (!ctm || _dpu_crtc_ctm_is_identity(ctm)) {
 				dspp->ops.setup_pcc(dspp, NULL);
 			} else {
 				_dpu_crtc_get_pcc_coeff(state, &cfg);
-- 
